                    AmiNFSv3 0.1 - documentation
                    ============================

  An NFSv3 client filesystem for AmigaOS 2.0+. Mounts a directory
  exported by a Linux machine or a NAS as an ordinary Amiga volume:
  it gets a device name, a volume name, a Workbench icon, and every
  program simply uses it as a disk.


  1. REQUIREMENTS
  ---------------

  - AmigaOS 2.0 or newer (developed and tested on 3.2). Any CPU:
    the binary is plain 68000 code.
  - A TCP/IP stack providing bsdsocket.library V4: Roadshow,
    AmiTCP, Miami, Genesis, or the Emu68 lwIP stack. AmiNFSv3 is
    developed against the Emu68 lwIP stack; reports from other
    stacks are welcome.
  - An NFSv3 server. A stock Linux kernel nfsd works as-is, and so
    does a Synology NAS. NFSv2-only servers are not supported (and
    modern Linux cannot serve v2 anyway - that is why this handler
    exists).
  - About 130 KB of memory per mounted device at default settings
    (handler code plus transfer buffers; the buffers follow the
    RSIZE/WSIZE options).


  2. INSTALLATION
  ---------------

  1. Copy nfs-handler to L:
  2. Copy NFSDismount to C:
  3. Copy the NFS0 DOSDriver (and its icon) somewhere sensible and
     edit its Startup line - host, export path, options (section 3).

  For mount-on-demand, keep the DOSDriver in SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers
  and mount it by path:

      Mount SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers/NFS0

  For automount at every boot, drag it into DEVS:DOSDrivers. The
  handler connects lazily - nothing touches the network until the
  first access - so it is safe to mount before the TCP stack is up.

  Several mounts run side by side: copy the file to NFS1, NFS2, ...
  and give each its own Startup line. Each device is a separate
  handler process with its own connection and its own volume.

  Note that `Mount NFS0: FROM file` does NOT accept DOSDriver-format
  files (it expects a classic MountList with a `NFS0:` stanza) -
  mount by direct path as shown above, or from DEVS:DOSDrivers by
  plain `Mount NFS0:`.


  3. THE DOSDRIVER AND ITS OPTIONS
  --------------------------------

  A minimal DOSDriver:

      Handler    = L:nfs-handler
      StackSize  = 16384
      Priority   = 5
      GlobVec    = -1
      Startup    = "192.168.1.10:/srv/amiga"

  Startup is `host:/export/path`, optionally followed by options,
  all inside one pair of quotes. The host is a dotted-quad address
  (name resolution is on the wish list). Options:

  VOLUME=name    The volume name - what Workbench shows and what
                 `name:` resolves to. Default: the last component
                 of the export path.

  UID=n GID=n    The identity sent with every request (AUTH_UNIX).
                 Default 1000/1000. Match this to the file owner on
                 the server, or export with all_squash (section 4)
                 and forget about it. For a Synology, use the ids
                 the NAS assigned your user (section 5).

  TZ=[+|-]H[:MM] Hours east of UTC. NFS timestamps are UTC and the
                 Amiga clock usually is not; TZ=+2 makes dates read
                 as local wall-clock time both ways (displayed
                 dates, and SetDate back). Default 0.

  RSIZE=n        Transfer chunk sizes in bytes, 4096..131072,
  WSIZE=n        default 65536/32768. Larger values cost memory
                 (the buffers are sized from these) and can help
                 fast setups; see section 7 before turning them up.

  DEPTH=1..8     Write/read pipelining: how many NFS requests the
                 handler keeps in flight during large transfers.
                 The default 1 is the plain synchronous handler -
                 one request at a time, the safe choice everywhere.
                 Higher values overlap the per-request latency and
                 can multiply large-file speed on fast stacks;
                 section 7 has measured combinations. Data safety
                 is identical at every depth: requests are matched
                 by transaction id, only contiguously-acknowledged
                 bytes are ever reported, short transfers re-issue
                 their tail, and a dead connection is replayed from
                 the last safe byte (NFS writes are idempotent, so
                 a replay can never corrupt).


  4. THE LINUX SERVER
  -------------------

  One line in /etc/exports:

      /srv/amiga 192.168.1.0/24(rw,insecure,no_subtree_check,all_squash,anonuid=1000,anongid=1000)

  then `exportfs -ra` (and `systemctl enable --now nfs-server` the
  first time). The pieces that matter:

  - `insecure` is required: Amiga stacks do not use privileged
    source ports.
  - `all_squash,anonuid=...` maps every access to one server user,
    which makes UID handling a non-issue for a single-user Amiga.
  - The subnet restriction means testing from the server itself
    via 127.0.0.1 is refused - test via the LAN address.

  If the server runs a firewall (ufw, firewalld, nftables), open
  TCP ports 111 (rpcbind), 2049 (nfsd) and the mountd port (see
  /etc/nfs.conf; often 20048) to your LAN. This is worth stating
  loudly: a default-deny host firewall silently eats the Amiga's
  connections while everything the server itself initiates still
  works - it looks exactly like a Amiga-side bug and is not.

      ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 111,2049,20048 proto tcp


  5. A NAS (SYNOLOGY)
  -------------------

  Enable NFS in DSM (Control Panel -> File Services), give the
  shared folder an NFS permission entry for your LAN, and mount:

      Startup = "192.168.1.20:/volume1/homes/you VOLUME=NAS UID=1026 GID=100"

  The UID/GID are the ones the NAS assigned your user - check with
  `id` over SSH, or by looking at any file you own from another
  machine. Nothing else is needed; a Synology speaks NFSv3 over TCP
  out of the box.


  6. DAILY USE
  ------------

  Mount as above. Both the device name (NFS0:) and the volume name
  (whatever VOLUME= says) work in paths once the handler has run;
  the volume appears on Workbench with a disk icon.

  Dismount:

      NFSDismount NFS0:

  This refuses while any file or lock on the volume is open, removes
  the volume from the system cleanly, and the handler exits. The
  device stays known: the next access to NFS0: starts a fresh
  handler, loading L:nfs-handler anew from disk - which also means
  replacing the handler binary never needs a reboot. Note that a
  relaunch reuses the Startup line the device was first mounted
  with; changed options in the DOSDriver file take effect at the
  next fresh Mount (after a reboot, if the device already exists).

  Filenames pass through byte-for-byte (Amiga Latin-1 on one side,
  usually UTF-8 on the other: ASCII names are always fine, accented
  characters will differ in look between the two worlds). Lookups
  are case-insensitive the Amiga way even though the server is
  case-sensitive: if exactly one entry matches regardless of case,
  it is used.


  7. PERFORMANCE
  --------------

  The defaults are chosen to behave everywhere: modest buffers, no
  pipelining. What to try, by setup - and measure, path quality
  varies more than settings do:

  Emu68 / PiStorm (lwIP stack):
      RSIZE=65536 WSIZE=16384 DEPTH=8
      Small chunks with a deep pipeline stream best on this stack;
      large WSIZE values combined with depth stall badly (a blocked
      send beyond the stack's real buffer waits on a coarse timer).
      Measured on an A1200/PiStorm against Linux over one Wi-Fi
      hop: 13-14 MB/s both directions with c:Copy, 65 MB/s reads
      with a 512 KB application buffer.

  Real NIC, fast stack (Roadshow + Zorro card):
      Start with defaults, then try RSIZE=131072 WSIZE=65536, then
      DEPTH=2..4. The NIC will likely be the ceiling.

  PCMCIA NIC or 8 MB-class machines:
      RSIZE=16384 WSIZE=16384 (small buffers, ~40 KB total). The
      NIC is the ceiling; depth changes little on a CPU-bound
      machine and nothing on the bus-bound part.

  Two structural notes. Directories of many small files are bound
  by per-file round trips (open/create/close each cost a few), not
  bandwidth - around 143 files/s read and 45 files/s written on the
  reference setup. And large-file speed follows the size of the
  reads an application makes: a program reading in 256 KB blocks
  gets several times the throughput of one reading 16 KB at a time,
  because the handler can only pipeline within one request.


  8. LIMITS AND HONEST NOTES
  --------------------------

  - File sizes: correct up to 4 GB. Above 2 GB the handler is
    exact, but AmigaDOS itself is not: FileInfoBlock sizes are
    signed 32-bit (some programs will show negative sizes), and
    Seek cannot address past 2 GB. Reading a 2.5 GB file start to
    finish works and was verified byte-exact.
  - Writes are UNSTABLE with a COMMIT when a file is closed (or on
    Flush). The server's write verifier is checked throughout: if
    the server restarts while a file is open, the Close fails with
    an error rather than pretending the data survived.
  - Protection bits: r and w/d map to the server mode (rwed on the
    Amiga side is mode 644, `Protect rd` gives 444). Directories
    always keep the server's execute bit so they stay traversable.
    The e bit and comments are not stored (SetComment returns an
    error rather than quietly dropping the comment).
  - Timestamps are stored in UTC on the server; use TZ=.
  - Symbolic and hard links on the server are not followed.
  - The host must currently be a dotted-quad address.
  - A server that goes away mid-operation costs a timeout (about
    ten seconds) and the operation fails with an error; the next
    access reconnects. A handler survives server reboots.


  9. TROUBLESHOOTING
  ------------------

  The handler reports its life through the kernel debug channel
  (KPrintF): mount events, the addresses it connects to, and every
  error path, prefixed `AmiNFSv3:`. Capture it with Sashimi (Aminet)
  or any serial-debug catcher. First things to check when a mount
  gives "device not mounted" errors:

  - Can anything else on the LAN reach the server's NFS? (A phone
    app or second PC rules the server in or out.)
  - The server firewall (section 4) - the classic silent failure.
  - The export line: `insecure` present? subnet right?
  - `showmount -e <server>` on the server itself lists what is
    actually exported where.


  10. HISTORY
  -----------

  0.1  First release. NFSv3 over TCP; read and write; UNSTABLE
       writes with COMMIT-on-close and verifier tracking; VOLUME,
       UID/GID, TZ, RSIZE/WSIZE, DEPTH mount options; pipelined
       transfers with fault-injection-tested recovery; NFSDismount;
       case-insensitive lookups; reboot-free handler upgrades.
